  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-FY 24 General Support Grant to Uganda</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Oxfam in Uganda&apos;s new Country Strategic Framework (2021-2030) reiterates Oxfam&apos;s commitment to fighting inequality working togetherwith others, it introduces a new pillar which focuses on women&apos;s rights and gender justice. A new organogram was recently approved and will be implemented during 2023/24 taking into consideration various internal changes in the confederation.

According to Oxfam internal rating, Uganda is not considered fragile and/or a risk country and yet the country hosts the largest number of refugees in Africa and continues to experience climate induced disasters and epidemics. Uganda also experiences proliferation of small arms and light weapons which cause instability and insecurity. This disadvantages Uganda in accessing internal resources.

The reduction of presence in neighbouring countries like Rwanda and Burundi may imply Uganda&#x2019;s increased engagement and role in establishing links with partners and networks in these countries to address humanitarian concerns of cross border nature.  For example, Oxfam Germany is supporting the continuation of a project on agroecology in Burundi by linking Oxfam in Uganda and partner (PELUM Uganda) to continue technical support provision to partners in Burundi through learning exchanges and future contracts management.

The fact that Ugandacontinues to operate as a full-fledged country program has implications on the scale and scope of our programming. While there has been an increase in CRA in the FY 2021/22, it still remains insufficientin supporting institutional costs required for effective programming. The country program has continued to receive unrestricted funding to complement programme financing from some partner affiliates. This support is anticipated to continue and possibly more partner affiliates will come on board. The Country Office also plans to increase its ability to raise costs recovery from restricted project funding.</narrative>
  </description>
